hdw4s (1.2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * An account has one desktop per machine. The "<user>:<session>" instance
    name, which gave one account a second concurrent desktop on the same
    machine, is no longer created; every command refuses it except "release",
    which is what retires one that already exists. An instance from an earlier
    version keeps running and keeps its port, and "hdw4s list" shows it as
    "legacy" rather than as an orphan, which is what it used to look like.
  * "hdw4s show" no longer prints a session number that was always 1.
  * New setting HDW4S_MICROPHONE, default no. The streaming server refuses
    microphone data on exactly one condition -- the feature disabled and locked
    -- which is what this has always shipped as. Setting it to yes lifts the
    refusal and keeps the lock, so the operator decides and a connected page
    cannot. It switches nobody's microphone on: the uplink starts off and
    muting stays the session's own toggle.
  * HDW4S_FRAMERATE can be set to a range. It has always meant a ceiling, and
    hdw4s-run-session turns a bare number into "N,8-N" so that it is one --
    a bare number sets only the initial rate and leaves the span at the
    streaming server's own 8 to 240, so a client could raise itself to 240 from
    the sidebar. An explicit range is passed through untouched, but "hdw4s set"
    typed the setting as a plain number and refused one, leaving editing the
    file by hand as the only way to use the form the runner supports.
  * The manual says why sendmail does not work in a session and what to do
    about it. ProtectSystem=strict makes the mail queue directory read-only, so
    postdrop cannot write and the message is lost with only a journal warning;
    SMTP to 127.0.0.1 is unaffected, because the mail system runs outside the
    session and a session shares the machine's network namespace. Installing an
    SMTP-submitting sendmail closes the gap for programs that shell out to it.
    Suggests: msmtp.
  * sudo works. The session unit's capability bounding set left out CAP_SETUID
    and CAP_SETGID, so sudo failed at "unable to change to root gid" before
    sudoers was read and before anyone was asked for a password -- on every
    machine running this package, since before it was released, and nobody
    chose it. It read as a security control and was not one: a setuid-root
    program reaches uid 0 whatever the bounding set says, and uid 0 owns every
    root-owned file by the ordinary permission bits. Measured under exactly
    that bounding set, a setuid-root binary read /etc/shadow and /etc/sudoers.
    All it blocked was the group change, which only sudo, su and login ask for,
    so it denied root to administrators and to nobody else. sudoers decides
    now, as it does everywhere else.
  * The reason the package needs GNOME 46 is stated as what it is. A session
    runs on a private D-Bus, so gnome-session cannot reach a systemd user
    manager and falls back to its own service manager -- and that fallback is
    what keeps the desktop inside the unit's control group, where stopping the
    unit can reap it. GNOME 49 removes the fallback and GNOME 50 refuses
    outright. This was documented as a limit on running a second desktop; it is
    a limit on the package.
  * HDW4S_ISOLATION=profile is justified by the case it actually serves, one
    home directory mounted on several machines. It no longer claims to be for
    coexisting with another desktop logged in on the same machine.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 10:00:00 -0700

hdw4s (1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A desktop can now be moved between devices. It belongs to one browser at a
    time; a second is told it is open elsewhere and offered a button to take it
    over, and the device that had it is told so and offered the same way back.
    A reload or a dropped connection is recognised as the same window returning
    rather than as a second device.
  * Turning a second client away no longer writes a traceback per attempt. A
    client that could not recognise the refusal reconnected several times a
    second; one stuck browser logged 17858 of them in two days.
  * A closed signalling socket can no longer stop a session. The desktop's read
    loop ran its callbacks inline and they sent on the same socket, so a close
    arriving mid-callback escaped into a bare exception handler and exited the
    process -- taking the desktop and every window open in it.
  * The updater checks what it downloaded against a recorded checksum, and says
    so plainly when it has nothing to check a hand-pinned version against.
  * The idle reaper counts the clients attached to a session rather than the
    session's own connections, so a desktop in use is no longer stopped.
  * "hdw4s show" reports whether session hand-over is in effect, which of its
    two halves is present, and the close codes a browser is turned away with.
  * "hdw4s list" and "hdw4s show" no longer report an authenticated session as
    unprotected when the caller cannot read its credential.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0700

